Review brief:
Prepare a focused review of how electrochemical oxidation, non-thermal plasma, and sonolysis should be matched to a defined PFAS-rich liquid stream.

Required components:
[{"requirement":"Compare the reaction environments, feed requirements, and PFAS structures for which each technology has been evaluated."},{"requirement":"Assess effects of concentration, salinity, co-contaminants, reactor operation, kinetics, and energy demand."},{"requirement":"Translate laboratory, pilot, and field evidence into conditional technology-selection criteria."}]

Task boundaries:
{"constraints":[{"material_violation_rule":"Triggers if one destructive technology is called universally superior without conditioning on feed, PFAS profile, reactor, endpoint, energy basis, and scale."},{"material_violation_rule":"Triggers if parent-compound loss alone is treated as complete destruction, defluorination, or mineralization."}],"task_boundaries":"Fit-for-purpose selection among electrochemical oxidation, non-thermal plasma, and sonolysis for PFAS-rich liquids; comparisons must remain feed-, endpoint-, reactor-, and scale-specific."}

Held-out anchor identifiers:
{"all_blocked_dois":["10.1016/j.coche.2023.100944"],"all_blocked_titles":["scaling up water treatment technologies for pfas destruction current status and potential for fit for purpose application"],"all_blocked_urls":["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coche.2023.100944"],"anchor_id":"AR-BLOTEVOGEL-2023","corpus_source":"2024 - Blotevogel - Scaling up water treatment technologies for PFAS destruction_ current status and potential for fit-for-purpose application.pdf","doi":"10.1016/j.coche.2023.100944","title":"Scaling up water treatment technologies for PFAS destruction: current status and potential for fit-for-purpose application","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coche.2023.100944"}

