        "text": "(Kewalra- mani et\u00a0al. 2022; Marsh et\u00a0al. 2024) The implosion of these ultrasound- generated bubbles creates a localized mineral- ization of PFAS while the bulk solution remains at lower temperature and pressure (Campbell and Hoffmann\u00a0 2015; Rodriguez- Freire et\u00a0al. 2015; Gole et\u00a0al. 2018; Kewalramani et\u00a0al. 2022). This is accomplished by achieving temperatures >1000 \u25e6 C and pressures ~1000 atm around the bubble, cre- ating a powerful destructive force (Awoyemi et\u00a0 al. 2024). High- frequency ultrasound has been demonstrated in previ- ous studies to mineralize PFAS in laboratory- scale reactors, creating fluoride and carbon dioxide without producing problematic by- products (Kewalramani et\u00a0 al. 2022). Fol- lowing the success of these bench level demonstrations of PFAS destruction in solid\u2013liquid slurries, high- frequency ultrasound technology can be evaluated as an energetically efficient treatment option for larger scale treatment, such as the on- site treatment of groundwater from contaminated sites. There are several regulatory drivers for the development of destruction technologies as the United States Environ- mental Protection Agency (USEPA) finalized a critical rule to designate two widely used PFAS: Perfluorooctanoic acid (PFOA) and Perfluorooctanesulfonic acid (PFOS)\u2014as haz- ardous substances under the Comprehensive Environmental"
