        "text": "completed, a final sample was taken, and the groundwater was pumped to the effluent tank. Each test has two sets of results, a set for Reactor 1 and a set for Reactor 2, except for Test 4 which only presents results from Reactor 2 and did not contain co- sampling of wet chemistry and metals. Additionally, each reactor has two chambers, Chamber A and Chamber B. A summary of tests conducted is presented in Table\u00a0 S1. Samples were collected periodically throughout testing. PFAS and TOC were collected at each sampling point, and additional parameters were only taken in one chamber per test. Samples were analyzed for PFAS, PFAS TOP, metals (iron, chromium, and manganese), anions (chloride, sulfate, and fluoride), TOC, COD, and adsorbable organic fluorine (AOF). Calculation of PFAS Degradation Efficiency Degradation efficiency serves as the primary metric to evaluate the effectiveness of PFAS destruction by ultrasound treatment. The degradation efficiency denotes the degree to which the PFAS were destroyed during the ultrasound treatment. A high degradation efficiency indicates a high destruction level of PFAS and thus an effective treatment process. The degradation efficiency (as sum of destruction of parent product) was assessed by measuring the decrease in PFAS concentrations. where DE, degradation efficiency; C o, Initial concentration"
