        "text": "was much more effective at destroying PFAS. Further test- ing of increased treatment time in a field- scale reactor is also recommended to determine the optimal treatment time for complete PFAS destruction. The small volume capacity of the system (100 L) remains a limitation, and so a larger scale study is recommended next following the successful demonstration presented in this study. It will be important to demonstrate that both efficiency and destructive performance can be main- tained as treatment volume increases to give this technology the best chance to effectively serve end users. Lastly, future research should consider evaluating gas- phase intermediate compounds that may be formed during ultrasonic treatment to fully evaluate the fluorine mass balance. Overall, this ultrasound technology was proven to be an effective treatment for the destruction of PFAS in highly contaminated groundwater within a source zone with little evidence of the formation of problematic by- products while achieving an order of magnitude decrease in concentration. We recommend additional experimentation to evaluate this technology to destroy pure AFFF or media contaminated with novel and/or replacement PFAS not evaluated in this study (Yan et\u00a0al. 2015; McCord et\u00a0al. 2020; Tenorio et\u00a0 al. 2020). Based on the decrease in AOF observed in this study, this technology should be broadly applicable to other PFAS of concern in addition to the groundwater"
