        "text": "released updated interim guidance on the destruction and disposal (D&D) of PFAS- containing materials, building on earlier guidance from 2020 (USEPA\u00a0 n.d.- b). The objectives of this study are to (i) implement an inno- vative ultrasound technology to treat PFAS- laden ground- water within source zones at impacted sites; (ii) evaluate and optimize critical parameters such as power density and residence time to achieve maximum degradation efficiency of PFAS; (iii) characterize the PFAS groundwater influent for PFAS, organics, and inorganics and assess whether the parameters may impact treatment efficiency; (iv) quantify the degradation efficiency for precursors, long- chain, and short- chain PFAS; and (v) monitor the mass balance of fluo- rine to determine if PFAS have been mineralized completely without creating PFAS as byproducts. The innovation in this application includes the use of high- frequency ultrasound combined with the number and orientation of the transduc- ers. Materials & Methods Design and Layout of 50- l Ultrasound Reactors and Field Treatment Trailer Upon successful testing and parameterization of the modular pilot scale reactor described in a recently pub - lished study, specialized 50- L ultrasound reactors were then designed to treat PFAS- impacted groundwater specifi- cally (Meegoda et\u00a0 al. 2025)."
